A16_SMSS_72_M016936_008                                                       
                                                                              
Apollo 16 Soil Mechanics                                                      
Lunar Self-Recording Penetrometer (LSRP) Page A-5                             
                                                                              
Plot of Penetration vs. Stress, Index No. 10 (fifth test)                     
                                                                              
The hand-drawn plot shows the stress applied at the penetrometer tip as       
a function of depth of penetration as recorded on the lunar drum.             
The plot was created using data from the Data Reduction Table                 
A16_SMSS_72_M016936_024, page B-10                                            
                                                                              
All 11 Apollo 16 Lunar Self-Recording Penetrometer (LSRP) runs were           
performed by lunar module pilot Charles Duke on the second EVA                
(extravehicular activity) on 22 April 1972. The final eight tests were        
done at station 10, at the ALSEP site at 8.9754 S latitude, 15.4981 E         
longitude near the landing site.  This was the final stop of the second       
EVA.  The runs at station 10 started with the fifth run overall and the       
LSRP index set to 10 (index 9 was skipped over before starting the            
station 10 runs.) All runs were done using the flight unit S/N 2004,          
lunar drum S/N 2008.                                                          
                                                                              
This run (fifth run, index 10) was done near the rover. It was performed      
at 23:00:48 UT (149:06:48 Mission Elapsed Time, or MET) using the 3.22        
square centimeter (0.5 square inch) cone.  On the plot, stress is given       
on the abscissa marked from 0 to 70 Newtons per square centimeter and 0       
to 15 ksf (1 ksf = 1000 pounds per square foot = 4.788 Newtons per            
centimeter squared) and penetration on the ordinate from 0 to 20              
centimeters. All quantities are given on a linear scale.                      
